{"h": {"measure": "R", "k": 100}, "sp": "nb", "rep": 111, "hyps": [{"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "none", "direction": "higher", "why": "When edge 2->3 is empty, a conv on 1->3 supplies a missing parametric path to the output (exps 48, 92 show large gains)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "If edge 1->3 is only pooling, a conv on 2->3 provides much-needed learnable capacity at the output (exps 96, 100)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "nor_conv_1x1", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing a conv on 0->1 with parameter-free pooling removes capacity feeding nodes 1-3, which hurts more than replacing none/skip (exps 7, 37)."}, {"edit_edge": 0, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 0, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Removing a pooling edge is harmless or helpful, while removing a conv on 0->1 consistently hurts (exps 10, 14 vs 54, 59, 75)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "none", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Deleting a 3x3 conv on an output edge removes a key learnable path, unlike deleting a pool (exps 28, 46 vs 89)."}, {"edit_edge": 4, "edit_op": "skip_connect", "cond_edge": 4, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Swapping a 3x3 conv for identity on 1->3 loses capacity, whereas swapping out pool or none is neutral or positive (exp 81 vs 5, 91)."}, {"edit_edge": 1, "edit_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "cond_edge": 1, "cond_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "direction": "lower", "why": "Replacing a 3x3 conv on 0->2 with pooling discards the strongest op on that edge (exp 1)."}, {"edit_edge": 5, "edit_op": "nor_conv_3x3", "cond_edge": 5, "cond_op": "avg_pool_3x3", "direction": "higher", "why": "Upgrading pooling to a 3x3 conv on 2->3 adds capacity, whereas upgrading an existing 1x1 conv gives little or nothing (exp 71 vs 72)."}, {"edit_edge": 3, 